\subsection{Thermoelectric Legs}

The wedge is divided into two separate regions in the azimuthal direction, one consisting of p type semiconductor material and other consisting of n type semiconductor material. They are shown in \cref{fig:TEC_elememt_iso} in light violet and light red colours respectively. Each leg in the stage can have arbitary lengths as long as they all add up to the final length. However, this increases the number of independant variables in the optimization problem. Therefore, we can perform a dimensional reduction by assuming that lengths of stages vary by some ratio. So let's define leng length ratio $f_L$ as,

\begin{equation}
  f_L = \frac{L_{i+1}}{L_i}
\end{equation}

where $L_i$ is the length of the TEC legs in stage $i$. Therefore, the length of each stage can be defined as:

\begin{equation}
  L_i = L_1 \cdot f_L^{(i-1)}
\end{equation}

where $L_1$ is the length of the TEC legs in the first stage. Since the radial insulator isn't included in the length, we can get a relationship between stage lengths and radius of the TEC array as:

\begin{equation*}
  \begin{aligned}
    r_{\text{base}} & = r_{\text{cyl}} + \sum_{i=1}^{N} L_i + (N+1) \cdot W_{\text{is}}                    \\
                    & =  r_{\text{cyl}} + \sum_{i=1}^{N} L_1 \cdot f_L^{(i-1)} + (N+1) \cdot W_{\text{is}} \\
                    & =  r_{\text{cyl}} + L_1 \cdot \frac{1-f_L^{N}}{1-f_L} + (N+1) \cdot W_{\text{is}}
  \end{aligned}
\end{equation*}

Solving for $L_1$ gives:
\begin{equation}
  L_1 = \frac{(r_{\text{base}} - r_{\text{cyl}} - (N+1) \cdot W_{\text{is}}) \cdot (1-f_L)}{1-f_L^{N}}
\end{equation}

This means that the lengths of all stages can be defined using only two independant variables, $f_L$ and $r_{\text{cyl}}$.

\subsection{Radial Insulator}

Radial insulators separate each TEC element in the radial direction. Each radial insulator is defined by its width $W_{\text{is}}$ which is constant throughout the entire radial TEC array. It has the same thickness as the TEC elements, $t_{\text{TEC}}$. Its indicated in dark green colour in \cref{fig:TEC_elememt_iso,fig:az_and_is_top}

\subsection{Azimuthal Insulator}

Azimuthal insulators separate the p-type and n-type legs in each TEC element, as well as between adjacent wedges. Each azimuthal insulator is defined by its with along the arc in the azimuthal direction, denoted as $W_{\text{az}}$. It also has the same thickness as the TEC elements, $t_{\text{TEC}}$. It is indicated in dark gray colour in \cref{fig:TEC_elememt_iso,fig:az_and_is_top}.

