\subsection{Interconnector}

Interconnector provides electrical contact between TEC elements within each stage. Its located near the cold side of the TE unit and connects the n-type leg of a wedge to the p-type leg of the same wedge.

\begin{figure}[H]
  \centering
  \begin{tikzpicture}
    \node[anchor=south west, inner sep=0pt] (img) at (0,0) {
      \includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{images/ic_iso.png}
    };

    %\draw[help lines, step=0.5cm] (0,0) grid (\columnwidth,7);

    \coordinate (ArcEnd) at (2.0, 3.0);
    \coordinate (Centre) at (2.5, 0);
    \coordinate (ArcStart) at (4.5, 2);
    \coordinate (A) at (2, 3.5);
    \coordinate (B) at (2.8, 5.2);
    \coordinate (C) at (5.0, 2.25);
    \coordinate (D) at (5.0, 2.8);

    \draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}, black, thin] (A) -- (B) node[midway, left, rotate=0] {$W_{\text{ic}}$};

    \draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}, black, thin] (C) -- (D) node[midway, right, rotate=0] {$t_{\text{ic}}$};

    \pic [draw, black, thin, "$\beta_{\text{ic}}$", angle eccentricity=0.9, angle radius=2.65cm,
    pic actions/.append style={{Stealth}-{Stealth}}] {angle = ArcStart--Centre--ArcEnd};
  \end{tikzpicture}
  \caption{Isometric view of Interconnector}
  \label{fig:ic_iso}
\end{figure}

Interconnector is parametrized by its width $W_{\text{ic}}$, thickness $t_{\text{ic}}$, and angle $\beta_{\text{ic}}$ it subtends at the center of the wedge, as shown in \cref{fig:ic_iso}. For the optmization purposes, all the interconnector dimensions are defined as fractions of the TEC element dimensions. Therefore, we can define:
\begin{equation}
  W_{\text{ic}} = f_{\text{ic,W}} \cdot L_{\text{TEC}} \quad ; \quad f_{\text{ic,W}} \in (0,1)
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
  t_{\text{ic}} = f_{\text{ic,t}} \cdot t_{\text{TEC}} \quad ; \quad f_{\text{ic,t}} \in (0,1)
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
  \beta_{\text{ic}} = f_{\text{ic,}\beta} \cdot \theta \quad ; \quad f_{\text{ic,}\beta} \in (0,1)
\end{equation}

The thickness is measured from the bottom of the TEC element to the top of the TEC array surface. Therefore it's not visible from the top view when $t_{\text{ic}} < t_{\text{TEC}}$.

\subsection{Outerconnector}

Outerconnector refeers to the electrical contact between TEC elements of adjacent wedges. Its located near the hot side of the TEC unit and connects the p-type leg of a wedge to the n-type leg of the adjacent wedge.

\begin{figure}[H]
  \centering
  \begin{tikzpicture}
    \node[anchor=south west, inner sep=0pt] (img) at (0,0) {
      \includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{images/oc_iso.png}
    };

    %\draw[help lines, step=0.5cm] (0,0) grid (\columnwidth,5);

    \coordinate (ArcStart) at (4.25, 2.5);
    \coordinate (Centre) at (4.5, 4.5);
    \coordinate (ArcEnd) at (6.5, 3.1);
    \coordinate (A) at (5.75, 4.1);
    \coordinate (B) at (6.25, 3.9);
    \coordinate (C) at (6.5, 3.3);
    \coordinate (D) at (6.5, 3.8);

    \draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}, black, thin] (A) -- (B) node[midway, above, rotate=0] {$W_{\text{oc}}$};

    \draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}, black, thin] (C) -- (D) node[midway, right, rotate=0] {$t_{\text{oc}}$};
    \pic [draw, black, thin, "$\beta_{\text{oc}}/2$", angle eccentricity=1.15, angle radius=2.35cm,
    pic actions/.append style={{Stealth}-{Stealth}}] {angle = ArcStart--Centre--ArcEnd};
  \end{tikzpicture}
  \caption{Isometric view of Outerconnector}
  \label{fig:oc_iso}
\end{figure}

Outerconnector is parametrized similarly to the interconnector, with width $W_{\text{oc}}$, thickness $t_{\text{oc}}$, and angle $\beta_{\text{oc}}$ it subtends at the center of the wedge, as shown in \cref{fig:oc_iso}. For the optmization purposes, all the outerconnector dimensions are defined as fractions of the TEC element dimensions. Therefore, we can define:

\begin{equation}
  W_{\text{oc}} = f_{\text{oc,W}} \cdot L_{\text{TEC}} \quad ; \quad f_{\text{oc,W}} \in (0,1)
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
  t_{\text{oc}} = f_{\text{oc,t}} \cdot t_{\text{TEC}} \quad ; \quad f_{\text{oc,t}} \in (0,1)
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
  \beta_{\text{oc}} = f_{\text{oc,}\beta} \cdot \theta \quad ; \quad f_{\text{oc,}\beta} \in (0,1)
\end{equation}

The thickness is measured from the top of the TEC element to the bottom of the TEC array surface, in contrast to the interconnector.

\section{Resistance Calculation}

To build the thermal network, the thermal resistances of each component must be calculated. Apart from that, the electrical resistances of the TEC elements must also be calculated to account for Joule heating effects.

\subsection{Resistor Arrangement}

\subsubsection{TEC Layer}

The calculation of thermal resistances depends on the direction of heat flow and the arrangement of components in that direction. In the radial TEC array, heat flows radially outward from the center of the chip to the outer edge of the TEC array.

Since there are many components, we need to pick a point to be used as a node in each TEC element. For convenience, we pick the junction between radial insulator, and central azimuthal insulator as the node point. This means that all components between two such points will be absorbed into a single thermal resistance. This resistance is used mainly to calculate the back conduction. The arrangement of resistors between two such nodes is shown in \cref{fig:TEC_resistor_diagram_iso}.


